    "id": "prop:heavy-tail-witness-validity",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "The constructed law \\(P^{\\star}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_4^{\\circ}\\), and at \\(P=P^{\\star}\\) it satisfies \\(E_P|D(O)|^{5/2}<\\infty\\) and \\(E_P|D(O)|^3=\\infty\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "P^{\\star}",
      "\\mathcal P_4^{\\circ}",
      "P",
      "E_P",
      "D",
      "O"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:interior-holder-class",
      "def:heavy-tail-witness",
      "lem:endpoint-moment-envelope"
    ],
    "justification": "The witness shows that the weak-moment argument is structural rather than cosmetic and rules out proofs that silently invoke a third moment.",
    "gap": "The positive-censoring frameworks of Westling et al. (2024) and Díaz et al. (2019) exclude this endpoint behavior by construction.",
    "consumer": "It gives referees and software authors a concrete stress test for variance and Studentization implementations.",
    "proof_tex": "Under \\(P^{\\star}\\), the density is \\(f\\equiv1\\), the propensity ranges from \\(0.4\\) to \\(0.6\\), the two hazards range within \\([1,1.75]\\), and\n\\(g_a(t,x)=e^{-xt/4}\\in[e^{-1/4},1]\\).  The derivatives through order three and the Lipschitz constants of the third derivatives of these functions are all strictly smaller than \\(20\\).  Every displayed band and Hölder-radius constraint consequently has positive slack.  Moreover,\n\\[\n \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\{(1-t)^2e^{-xt/4}\\}\n =-e^{-xt/4}\\left\\{2(1-t)+\\frac{x}{4}(1-t)^2\\right\\}\\le0,\n\\]\nthe survival starts at one and decreases continuously to zero, and the stated conditional construction supplies consistency, treatment exchangeability, and independent censoring.  Hence \\(P^{\\star}\\in\\mathcal P_4^{\\circ}\\).\n\nThe \\(5/2\\)-moment assertion follows immediately from lem:endpoint-moment-envelope.  To prove that the third moment is infinite, fix either arm and write \\(r=1-t\\).  The bands, or the explicit exponential formula, give constants \\(c_1,c_2>0\\) such that for all sufficiently small \\(r\\),\n\\[\n \\frac{H_a(1-r,x)}{G_a(1-r\\mid x)}\\ge\\frac{c_1}{r},\n \\qquad\n \\int_0^{1-r}\\frac{H_a(t,x)\\lambda_a(t,x)}{G_a(t\\mid x)}\\,dt\n \\le c_2\\log(1/r). \\tag{38}\n\\]\nOn \\(A=a\\) and an observed event at \\(U=1-r\\), the event jump in the corresponding arm component of \\(D\\) has absolute size at least \\(c_1/(\\pi_a(X)r)\\), whereas all remaining terms are bounded by a constant plus \\(c_2\\log(1/r)\\).  Thus, for some \\(r_0,c_3>0\\), \\(|D|\\ge c_3/r\\) on such events with \\(r<r_0\\).  Their conditional density is\n\\[\n S_a(1-r\\mid x)G_a(1-r\\mid x)\\lambda_a(1-r,x)\\,dr\\ge c_4r^2\\,dr\n\\]\nfor a constant \\(c_4>0\\).  Treatment probabilities are uniformly positive, so Tonelli's theorem and (38) yield\n\\[\n E_{P^{\\star}}|D(O)|^3\n \\ge c_5\\int_0^{r_0}r^{-3}r^2\\,dr\n =\\infty.\n\\]\nThis proves both moment claims without invoking a third- or fourth-moment bound.",
